      "id": "2088835568264438235",
      "text": "DeepSeek did it again.\n\nThey quietly published a paper that fixes THE fundamental bug in every transformer ever trained.\n\nEvery major AI model you use today is relies on something called the residual connection.\n\nSince ResNets in 2016, neural networks have used simple, additive shortcuts to pass information from one layer to the next. \n\nIt’s what allowed deep learning to go from a few layers to hundreds without exploding or vanishing gradients.\n\nIt became the invisible law of AI architecture.\n\nWhen you try to scale up standard residual streams or use \"Hyper-Connections\" to expand network capacity, things break down catastrophically. \n\nWithout strict constraints, information starts compounding.\n\nSignals amplify. Matrix values spiral out of control. Amplification factors hit 3,000x in practice.\n\nTraining crashes. Loss curves explode.\n\nEvery major lab knew the architecture was hitting a wall.\n\nThen DeepSeek quietly published a paper fixing it at the mathematical root.\n\nThey call it Manifold-Constrained Hyper-Connections (mHC).\n\nInstead of letting information streams multiply blindly, DeepSeek used a 1946 mathematical concept, the Sinkhorn-Knopp algorithm, to project the mixing matrices onto a constrained manifold.\n\nEvery single stream is forced to balance out.\n\nNothing explodes. Nothing vanishes.\n\nThe math guarantees total preservation.\n\nThe instability dropped from a 3,000x explosion down to a controlled 1.6x.\n\nThey engineered the hardware execution down to the metal, fusing kernels and slashing memory traffic so it runs at massive scale without dragging down inference speeds.",
